  "worked_solution": "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061y\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{x}{y}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(2,1),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(1)\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{2}{1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =0\\,\\text{d}x+2\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(2+\u0394x,1+\u0394y),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\n\u0394f\u22480\\,\u0394x+2\\,\u0394y\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n(1.81)\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}\u2061(0.91)=f(1.81,0.91),\n$$\n\nso we want to move to the point $(1.81,0.91).$\n\nMoving from $(2,1)$ to $(1.81,0.91),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u0394x & =1.81-2=-0.19, \\\\ \u0394y & =0.91-1=-0.09.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u0394f & \u22480\\cdot (-0.19)+2\\cdot (-0.09) \\\\  & \u22480-0.18 \\\\  & \u2248-0.18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that the value of $f$ decreases by about $0.18.$ Consequently, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1.81,0.91) & \u2248f(2,1)-0.18 \\\\ (1.81)\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}\u2061(0.91) & \u22482\\operatorname{ln}\u20611-0.18 \\\\  & \u22480-0.18 \\\\  & \u2248-0.18.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
